# Lore AMI Build Guide

Generated by `fabrica lore ami build` for Lore **{{ .Version }}** in `{{ .Region }}`.

This directory is a backend-neutral AMI definition plus two thin build adapters.
Image Builder is the recommended path; Packer uses the same install and bake
verification scripts. `fabrica lore create` consumes only the resulting AMI ID.

## AMI Contract

| Requirement | Value |
|---|---|
| Base OS | {{ .Contract.BaseOS }} |
| Architecture | {{ .Contract.Architecture }} |
| Binary | `{{ .Contract.BinaryPath }}` and `{{ .Contract.CommandPath }}` |
| Service | `{{ .Contract.ServiceName }}.service`, enabled at bake time |
| Runtime config | `{{ .Contract.ConfigDir }}/local.toml`, created by Fabrica cloud-init |
| Health endpoint | `http://127.0.0.1:{{ .Contract.HTTPPort }}{{ .Contract.HealthPath }}` |

Do not bake credentials, a store, or a generated Lore configuration. The unit is
enabled but waits for Fabrica cloud-init to create `local.toml` before starting.

## Generated Files

- `component.yaml` — Image Builder adapter. Replace `REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_BUCKET`
  with a private S3 bucket that holds the studio-supplied Lore distribution.
- `image-builder-recipe.json` — Image Builder recipe. Replace
  `REPLACE_WITH_CUSTOM_COMPONENT_ARN` after creating the component.
- `install-lore.sh` — shared AMI install contract used by both adapters.
- `verify-lore-ami-bake.sh` — shared static bake verification used by both adapters.
- `verify-lore-ami-runtime.sh` — post-boot verification after Fabrica writes config.
{{- if .IncludePacker }}
- `packer.pkr.hcl` — Packer adapter that executes the shared install and bake checks.
{{- end }}
- `build-guide.md` — this guide.

## Prerequisites

Your studio must obtain the Epic-licensed `loreserver` distribution separately.
Fabrica does not download it. Prepare a directory whose root contains an executable
`loreserver` file and any required companion files; do not include credentials.

For complete IAM, private-network, image-pipeline, launch, health, S3-store, and
cleanup instructions, follow the repository runbook: `docs/lore-ami.md`.

## Image Builder (Recommended)

1. Upload the licensed distribution to `s3://YOUR_PRIVATE_BUCKET/loreserver-bin/`.
   The Image Builder instance profile needs `s3:ListBucket` and `s3:GetObject` only
   for that prefix.
2. Replace `REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_BUCKET` in `component.yaml`.
3. Create the component, substitute its ARN in `image-builder-recipe.json`, then
   create the recipe and pipeline as described in `docs/lore-ami.md`.
4. Start the pipeline. It runs `verify-lore-ami-bake.sh` before it distributes an AMI.
5. Launch through `fabrica lore create`, run `fabrica lore status`, and run
   `verify-lore-ami-runtime.sh` through SSM before recording the AMI as known-good.

## Packer (Alternative)

Run Packer from this generated directory with the same licensed distribution:

```bash
packer init packer.pkr.hcl
packer fmt -check packer.pkr.hcl
packer validate -var "lore_source_dir=/absolute/path/to/lore-bin" packer.pkr.hcl
packer build -var "lore_source_dir=/absolute/path/to/lore-bin" packer.pkr.hcl
```

Packer copies that directory to `/tmp/lore-bin/` and invokes `install-lore.sh` and
`verify-lore-ami-bake.sh`; it does not maintain a separate install definition.

## Record Verification

Do not mark an AMI usable from a successful bake alone. Record its AMI ID, region,
base image, Lore/UE source version, both backend verification results, and cleanup
result in the known-good table in `docs/lore-ami.md` only after boot and health pass.
